  "description": "The application **my\u2011app.service** will be launched under the user *appuser* with three instances, each allocated 1.5\u202fCPU units, 512\u202fMiB of memory and 1\u202fGiB of disk space. No custom executor is defined, so the default executor will be used. Restart back\u2011off is set to start after 10\u202fseconds and increase by a factor of\u202f2.0 on successive failures. The upgrade strategy permits up to 20\u202f% over\u2011capacity during a rollout while requiring at least 50\u202f% of the tasks to remain healthy. The service depends on the relative path **../lib**, pulls resources from **http://example.com/store**, and will fetch the archive **http://example.com/resource.tar.gz** before starting.\n\nThe container runs Docker with the image **nginx:latest**, forcing the image pull on each deployment. It uses the BRIDGE network in non\u2011privileged mode and maps container port\u202f80 to host port\u202f8080 (TCP) with a service port of\u202f9000; the host also reserves ports\u202f8080 and\u202f8443 for the application, but ports are not required to be pre\u2011allocated. No additional Docker parameters or placement constraints are specified. A volume mounts the host path **/data/nginx/log** to **/var/log/nginx** inside the container with read\u2011write access. Environment variables set are **ENVIRONMENT=production** and **LOG_LEVEL=info**, and the task is labeled as belonging to the *web* project and the *backend* team. Health checking is enabled via an HTTP probe on **/health** (port index\u202f0) with a 30\u2011second grace period, 10\u2011second intervals, a 5\u2011second timeout, and it will tolerate up to three consecutive failures; HTTP\u202f1xx responses are not ignored."
